ed per-call diagnostics.\" And \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" That suggests diagnostics for all calls, not just accepted. But \"accepted call IDs\" is separate.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll design the solution to:\n\n- Validate case: must have \"threshold\" (float) and \"calls\" (list). Each call must have required fields.\n- Compute for each call: gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call; normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (since prediction_tokens >0 always from data, but handle zero by using max(prediction_tokens,1) as safeguard).\n- Determine acceptance: parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n- Collect accepted call IDs in order.\n- Build diagnostics list: for each call (original order), include call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, gain, normalized_gain (rounded to some decimals? The instruction says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". Probably round to a reasonable number of decimal places, maybe 5 or 6? Since input losses have 8 decimals. I'll round to 5 decimal places maybe.\n- Also include \"accepted\" boolean? Not specified but may be useful.\n- Return a dict with \"accepted\" (list of strings) and \"diagnostics\" (list of dicts).\n\nBut wait: the phrase \"normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order\" suggests these are keys in the result. Possibly the diagnostics should contain \"normalized_weight\" (the normalized gain), \"stronger_counterfactual\" (maybe the value of loss_without or loss_with_call that is larger? Or the gain?), \"inclusive_threshold\" (the threshold used), \"stable_order\" (maybe an indicator that order is preserved). But this seems too many. Alternatively, these are the properties that the solution must adhere to, not output keys.\n\nGiven the strict requirement to return only JSON-serializable values with exact named fields, I need to infer the expected output structure from typical similar tasks. Since the fixture is provided, maybe the expected output is known. But not in the prompt. I'll have to make a reasonable assumption.\n\nAnother idea: The instruction says \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string\" with key \"implementation\". So my response will be a JSON object with one key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing the Python code. That code defines `solve(case)`.\n\nI'll implement as described, with validation and returning accepted and diagnostics. I'll include \"normalized_gain\" as diagnostic field. I'll also round to 5 decimal places using round(x,5). Use standard library only.\n\nMake sure not to mutate case.\n\nI'll produce code that raises ValueError if case missing required fields.\n\nNow write the JSON response.\n\nNote: The Python code string must be escaped properly in JSON. I'll write it as a multi-line string with proper indentation, then escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's draft the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case structure\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be numeric\")\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    required_fields = {'call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable'}\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for c in calls:\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        if not required_fields.issubset(c.keys()):\n            raise ValueError(\"missing required field in call\")\n        call_id = c['call_id']\n        loss_without = c['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = c['loss_with_call']\n        pred_tokens = c['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = c['parseable']\n        # compute\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # normalize by at least one token\n        tokens = max(pred_tokens, 1)\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\n        accepted_flag = parseable and norm_gain >= threshold\n        # round diagnostics\n        diag = {\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'loss_without_call': loss_without,\n            'loss_with_call': loss_with,\n            'gain': round(gain, 5),\n            'normalized_gain': round(norm_gain, 5),\n            'prediction_tokens': pred_tokens,\n            'parseable': parseable,\n            'accepted': accepted_flag\n        }\n        diagnostics.append(diag)\n        if accepted_flag:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n    # return result\n    return {\n        'accepted': accepted,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nBut does it need to include \"threshold\" in diagnostics? Possibly. Also \"inclusive_threshold\" maybe as a key? The instruction says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Not clear.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll stick with above. Also note that the instruction says \"normalized_weights\" might be a field? I'll not add extra fields beyond what is obvious.\n\nTo be safe, maybe include \"threshold\" in diagnostics? But not required. I'll keep minimal set.\n\nAlso, ensure rounding is consistent. Use round(x,5) as specified? It says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". I'll round gain and normalized_gain.\n\nNow produce JSON. The string must be a single line inside JSON value. I'll convert the Python code to a string with escaped newlines (\\\\n) and quotes.\n\nI'll write the response directly as JSON.\n\nLet's craft the final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1762,"completion_tokens":2979,"total_tokens":4741,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2515},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}